    "q-213097": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213097",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "|  | $N$ | $Z$ | $Q$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $N$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $Z$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $Q$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nThe adjacency matrix of the relation $R,$ defined on the set $\\{N,Z,Q\\},$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(N,Z)$\n\nII. $(Q,Q)$\n\nIII. $(Z,Z)$",
      "worked_solution": "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}\\}$ and $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}\\}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (or table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(N,Z)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $N$-row and the $Z$-column.\n\n- The pair $(Q,Q)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the adjacency matrix has $1$ on the intersection of the $Q$-row and the $Q$-column.\n\n- The pair $(Z,Z)$ does not belong to the relation since the adjacency matrix has $0$ on the intersection of the $Z$-row and the $Z$-column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
