    "q-213119": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213119",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Consider the sets $A=\\{-2,-1,0\\}$ and $B=\\{0,1,2\\},$ and the relation $R=\\{(x,y)\\in A\\times B\\,:\\,x=y-1\\}.$\nFill in the missing values in the adjacency matrix of the relation:\n\n|  | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |\n| $-1$ | {{field-3}} | $0$ | {{field-4}} |\n| $0$ | $0$ | {{field-5}} | $0$ |",
      "worked_solution": "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}\\}$ and $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}\\}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$ with $x=y-1.$\n\nLet's consider each row in our table.\n\n- If $x=-2,$ then there is no value of $y\\in B$ that satisfies $x=y-1.$\n\n- If $x=-1,$ then the value $y=0$ satisfies $x=y-1.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(-1,0),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $1$st column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=0,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x=y-1.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(0,1),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $3$rd row and the $2$nd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\nAll the other cells in the matrix must be filled with zeros. Therefore, we have the following adjacency matrix:\n\n|  | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
