    "q-213121": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213121",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Consider the sets $A=\\{1,4,8\\}$ and $B=\\{0,6,12\\},$ and the relation $R=\\{(x,y)\\in A\\times B\\,:\\,x∣y\\}.$\nFill in the missing values in the adjacency matrix of the relation:\n\n|  | $0$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n| $4$ | {{field-4}} | $0$ | {{field-5}} |\n| $8$ | {{field-6}} | {{field-7}} | $0$ |",
      "worked_solution": "If a relation $R$ is defined over $A=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{m}\\}$ and $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},…{b}_{n}\\}$ of cardinalities $m$ and $n,$ respectively, the *adjacency matrix* of $R$ is a $m\\times n$-matrix (table), where the element ${r}_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column is given by\n\n$$\n{r}_{ij}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,\\text{if }{a}_{i}\\,R\\,{b}_{j}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$ with $x∣y.$\n\nLet's consider each row in our table.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the values $y=0,6,12$ satisfy $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(1,0),$ $(1,6),$ and $(1,12),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $1$st column, in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $2$nd column, and in the intersection of the $1$st row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the values $y=0,12$ satisfy $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(4,0)$ and $(4,12),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $1$st column and in the intersection of the $2$nd row and the $3$rd column in the adjacency matrix.\n\n- If $x=8,$ then the value $y=0$ satisfies $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,0),$ and we write $1$ in the intersection of the $3$rd row and the $1$st column in the adjacency matrix.\n\nAll the other cells in the matrix must be filled with zeros. Therefore, we have the following adjacency matrix:\n\n|  | $0$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $8$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
